{"title":"Regular Expression Matching","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"Implement a tiny regular-expression matcher. You are given a text `s` and a pattern `p`. The pattern supports two special characters:\n\n- `.` matches **any single** character.\n- `*` matches **zero or more** copies of the character immediately before it. That preceding character is either a lowercase letter or `.` (so `.*` matches any string, including the empty one).\n\nThe match must cover the **entire** text `s`, not just a part of it. Print `true` if the whole of `s` matches `p`, otherwise `false`.\n\nIt is guaranteed that `*` never appears as the first character of `p` and never directly follows another `*`.\n\n**Follow-up:** a 2026 Zoho online assessment reportedly used `+` (one or more of the preceding character) together with `*` instead of the `.`/`*` pair. After solving this version, think about how you would adapt your transition for `+`.","inputFormat":"The first line contains the text `s`.\nThe second line contains the pattern `p`.","outputFormat":"Print `true` if `p` matches the entire string `s`, otherwise print `false`.","constraints":["1 ≤ |s| ≤ 1000","1 ≤ |p| ≤ 1000","s consists of lowercase English letters","p consists of lowercase English letters, `.` and `*`; every `*` follows a letter or `.`"],"examples":[{"input":"zzzy\nz*.y*","output":"true","explanation":"`z*` takes the first three `z`. Then `.` matches the final `y`, and `y*` matches zero characters. Everything is consumed, so the answer is true."},{"input":"cabd\nc.*bb*","output":"false","explanation":"`c` matches `c`. `.*` must stop before a `b`, which can only be at index 2. `b` matches it and `b*` matches nothing, but the final `d` is left unmatched. No other split works, so the answer is false."},{"input":"k\n.*","output":"true","explanation":"`.*` can match any string, including the single character `k`."}],"tests":[{"input":"a\na","output":"true"},{"input":"a\n.","output":"true"},{"input":"a\nb","output":"false"